  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Initial Design**\n\nI\'ve just reviewed the complete HTML designs for the four screens. Screen 1, \"Canchas,\" presents the core explore courts feature, starting with the header including the logo, the app name, and the hours the courts are open. I\'m focusing on the overall layout and initial elements.\n\n\n**Analyzing Screen Elements**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of Screen 1, the \"Canchas\" view. I\'m focusing on the avatar profile badge with its greeting, the dynamic banner for matchmaking opportunities, and the filter chips that allow quick searches. The court cards, each with pricing, features, and availability, are next on my list. I also am paying attention to the \"Reserva Rel\303\241mpago\" drawer and the bottom tab bar.\n\n\n**Examining Booking Details**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the \"Reservar\" screen, concentrating on the booking flow. The weekly calendar carousel immediately caught my eye, and I\'m looking at how users select dates and sessions. I\'m focusing on the court selection options, the duration switcher, and how time slots are displayed in the grid. I am also paying attention to the bottom sheet drawer and the booking details it will display, particularly how costs and notes are handled.\n\n\n**Assessing Payment Integration**\n\nI\'m now closely reviewing the \"Partidos\" screen, specifically the payment features. I\'m focusing on the \"Subir Comprobante de Pago\" functionality, including the bank details display, the file upload zone, and the \"Enviar Comprobante\" button. I\'m also assessing the \"Invitar Jugadores\" and \"Cancelar Turno\" buttons and their associated actions. The hero match card with countdown and status displays are also being examined.\n\n\n**Evaluating Club Administration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on Screen 4, the \"Club\" view, and particularly the \"Modo Canchero\" admin features. I\'m examining the role switcher bar and how the display changes between \"Vista Jugador\" and \"Modo Canchero\". I\'m looking at the Club Location Card and the interactive accordions for norms and services. I\'m also assessing the visual amenity teasers and the persistent WhatsApp trigger.\n\n\n**Implementing Real-Time Management**\n\nI\'m now focused on the \"Modo Canchero\" features for real-time court management. I\'m prioritizing the active turn card, the swift call and WhatsApp integrations. A smooth slider for payment confirmation is what I\'m looking at. I\'m also analyzing the instant blocking functionality for maintenance or telephone bookings, including court selection, reason, and confirmation, and thinking about walk-in bookings and their schema for input. Finally, I\'m examining the rapid day stats display.\n\n\n**Defining Database Schemas**\n\nI\'ve just structured the database schema to manage users, with fields for full names, phone numbers, avatars, roles, and timestamps. I\'ve defined the courts table to include names, types, and rates, plus photos and descriptions. Finally, I\'ve created the amenities catalog to manage services, including pricing, availability, and images.\n\n\n**Defining Booking Tables**\n\nI\'ve just defined the `booking_status` enum with states like \'pending_payment\' and \'confirmed\'. I\'m designing the `bookings` table with a focus on core fields: `court_id`, `user_id`, and booking details like start and end times, total price, and the booking status. I\'m also including `guest_name` and `guest_phone` for manual entries, and a `payment_proof_url` along with `notes`. The constraints ensure valid durations, preventing overlap of court schedules.\n\n\n**Structuring App Architecture**\n\nI\'m now focusing on establishing a strict architecture for the application. `App.tsx` will exclusively handle routing, ensuring no business logic resides there. All Firebase interactions and API calls will be centralized in `src/apiService.ts`, including user profile management, login procedures, and routine generation. Reusable UI elements will reside in `src/components/`, while screens will reside in `src/screens/`.\n\n\n**Defining Screen Hierarchy**\n\nI\'m now building the screen structure. I\'m focusing on screen components that will take up the entire visible view and organize smaller components. The plan is to create `CanchasScreen`, `ReservarScreen`, `PartidosScreen`, and `ClubScreen`. I\'m also planning to create a `src/types.ts` for type definition.\n\n\n"
