  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing RLS Implementation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the PostgreSQL Row Level Security (RLS) policies. I\'ve successfully implemented policies for the `profiles` table: users can view and update their own profiles using the `auth.uid()` function to check their unique identifier. I\'m now turning my attention to the `courts` and `amenities_catalog` tables and exploring how to apply RLS in those contexts.\n\n\n**Refining Booking Rules**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the `bookings` table RLS. I\'ve defined rules for customer booking access, focusing on how club admins need slightly different privileges. I\'m prioritizing privacy, especially in the public booking view. My aim is to enforce the principle that customers see only blocked time slots, not customer-specific booking details in the public views, ensuring compliance between the backend and frontend views.\n\n\n**Documenting Access Control**\n\nI\'m now documenting these policies. I\'m adding SQL migration files to clearly define RLS policies and table schemas within the codebase, such as `supabase/schema.sql`. I\'m also revising `src/apiService.ts` to enforce the implemented RLS rules during data access, especially within the `fetchBookingsForUser()` function.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_384224"
