    thinking: "**Analyzing Function Design**\n\nI\'m currently focused on evaluating the PostgreSQL stored function provided. I\'ve begun dissecting the function\'s structure, specifically the input parameters and return type. The `p_court_id` and `p_date` parameters appear straightforward. I\'m taking a closer look at `p_slot_duration_minutes` with the default, as well as the structure of the returned table\'s columns: `slot_start` and `slot_end`.\n\n\n**Interpreting Function Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the core logic. This stored function generates available court slots for a given date and court, taking slot duration into account. I\'m focusing on the `WHILE` loop and the date-time calculations, specifically how it iterates through time slots between club opening and closing. I\'m examining the `NOT EXISTS` condition for booking availability.\n\n\n**Evaluating Implementation Approach**\n\nI\'ve been thinking about the security implications, especially the use of `SECURITY DEFINER` and its role in allowing public access to availability checks without compromising data integrity. This approach seems sound. Now, I\'m focusing on the translation to TypeScript. I\'m planning to implement a similar iterative process with time slots, ensuring it replicates the SQL query\'s logic accurately, including the booking status check for \'confirmed\' or \'pending_payment\'. I will be sure to save the function definition in the schema, and call it from the service.\n\n\n"
